WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.690
In Hollywood, the state of a big-budget movie
can change at the drop of a hat.

00:00:04.690 --> 00:00:07.000
Take 2013's World War Z, for instance.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.390
After a cavalcade of behind-the-scenes drama,
it's a wonder that the project even made it

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.390
to theaters.

00:00:12.390 --> 00:00:17.140
But once it did, the movie proved its worth
by raking in roughly $540 million dollars

00:00:17.140 --> 00:00:18.140
worldwide.

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:22.339
Similarly, the World War Z sequel has changed
directors, screenwriters, has been assigned

00:00:22.339 --> 00:00:26.769
multiple release dates, and was even pulled
entirely from Paramount's release calendar.

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:29.749
So what exactly is the current state of the
zombie sequel?

00:00:29.749 --> 00:00:33.090
Here's everything we know about the follow-up
to World War Z.

00:00:33.090 --> 00:00:34.670
Brad's back

00:00:34.670 --> 00:00:37.760
According to Variety, Pitt is returning for
World War Z 2.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:41.850
Given the first film's success, that may not
necessarily seem like a surprise, but Pitt

00:00:41.850 --> 00:00:44.300
hasn't really made a habit of appearing in
sequels.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.960
This time, regardless of the many projects
he has on his plate, he's remained the star

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.260
and producer all along.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:53.120
And it's a good thing he has his hands so
tightly on the reins — without Pitt leading

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:56.760
the way, World War Z 2 would probably fall
apart completely.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:58.320
Mireille Enos

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:02.530
Another cast member returning is Mireille
Enos, who played Pitt's wife in the original

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:03.530
film.

00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:07.009
In a 2016 interview with Digital Spy, Enos
confirmed her return, saying,

00:01:07.009 --> 00:01:10.970
"There's been some shifting [with directors]
and so the schedule's a bit up in the air,

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:13.200
but it's definitely happening, and I get to
be in it."

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:17.700
When you look at Enos' recent work, it's clear
she's been very busy in front of the camera.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.130
Fortunately, it sounds like she's still committed
to making it work once World War Z 2 production

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:23.909
is up and running.

00:01:23.909 --> 00:01:24.909
Rewrites

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:29.920
In 2014, a year after World War Z hit theaters,
The Wrap reported that screenwriter Steven

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.640
Knight was brought on board to pen the sequel.

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:36.740
Not long after Knight delivered a draft to
Paramount, Variety reported that Dennis Kelly,

00:01:36.740 --> 00:01:41.780
the creator of the U.K. conspiracy thriller
series Utopia was hired to deliver a rewrite.

00:01:41.780 --> 00:01:45.880
The highly anticipated sequel may be hitting
the same type of speed bumps as the original

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:49.840
film, but it's a good sign that the team behind
it appears to be focused on the quality of

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:51.159
the story.

00:01:51.159 --> 00:01:52.389
Scheduling conflicts

00:01:52.389 --> 00:01:55.729
The film's production schedule has definitely
not been easy to lock down.

00:01:55.729 --> 00:02:00.740
In January 2017, after multiple rewrites,
The Hollywood Reporter reported Paramount's

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.340
move to pull the project from its release
calendar.

00:02:03.340 --> 00:02:08.369
This came after multiple efforts to push back
the film's tentative release date from 2016

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:10.709
to a 2017 summer release.

00:02:10.709 --> 00:02:15.420
According to Variety, production was once
again rescheduled to late 2018 so Pitt could

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:19.989
work on Quentin Tarantino's Charles Manson-inspired
film Once Upon a Time in Hollywood.

00:02:19.989 --> 00:02:24.610
If all goes according to plan, audiences probably
won't see another World War Z movie until

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.819
2020 — and that's just a guesstimate.

00:02:27.819 --> 00:02:28.819
Fincher's entrance

00:02:28.819 --> 00:02:31.690
Luckily, after the major setback of losing
director J.A.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:35.890
Bayona — who moved on to direct the next
Jurassic World film — David Fincher jumped

00:02:35.890 --> 00:02:37.489
into the zombie fray.

00:02:37.489 --> 00:02:40.989
According to Variety, Pitt had been doing
his best to persuade Fincher to take over

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:42.870
directing duties for some time.

00:02:42.870 --> 00:02:47.130
Pitt and Fincher had developed a solid professional
relationship over the years — working together

00:02:47.130 --> 00:02:50.980
on Fight Club, Seven, and The Curious Case
of Benjamin Button.

00:02:50.980 --> 00:02:55.860
Fincher hasn't directed a sequel since 1992's
Alien 3, and while his addition to the project

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:59.920
brings high expectations, his attention to
story and characters would prove a welcome

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:00.920
departure.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.140
The director told Collider in 2017,

00:03:02.140 --> 00:03:06.709
"I don't care if the whole scene is five pages
of two people in a car sipping coffee from

00:03:06.709 --> 00:03:10.640
paper cups as long as there's a fascinating
power dynamic and I learn something about

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:11.670
them."

00:03:11.670 --> 00:03:12.969
Not a cash grab

00:03:12.969 --> 00:03:17.200
When Fincher appeared on the Empire podcast
to promote Mindhunter, he supplied a brief

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:19.390
update on the sequel's status.

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:23.140
Explaining their process in dissecting novelist
Max Brooks' story, Fincher said,

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:29.680
"We're hoping to get a piece of material that's,
you know, a reason to make a movie, not an

00:03:29.680 --> 00:03:31.420
excuse to make a movie."

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:35.069
This may seem like a no-brainer, but in this
day and age where big-budget blockbusters

00:03:35.069 --> 00:03:39.261
focus more on jaw-dropping action sequences
than character development or story, it's

00:03:39.261 --> 00:03:40.590
an important detail.

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:42.939
The director further explained to Little White
Lies,

00:03:42.939 --> 00:03:44.579
"A lot of stones have been laid.

00:03:44.579 --> 00:03:48.159
We're just deconstructing it right now against
the mythology that exists to see where we

00:03:48.159 --> 00:03:49.159
can go."

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:50.159
Thanks for watching!

